00:05:18.128 --> 00:05:20.170
VINCENT RICCI: So again
my name is Vincent Ricci.

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:24.648
I am the lead cloud engineer
at Colgate-Palmolive.

00:05:24.648 --> 00:05:26.190
And today we're
going to go over some

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.830
of the foundational elements
we had to put in place

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.435
prior migrating to GCP.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:38.930
So the scope of the project
was simple on paper.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:43.430
We had to migrate a workload
of 1,200 Windows machines

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.360
and stay within the lift and
shift migration strategy.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.000
We wanted to avoid
any re-architecting

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.250
or replatforming
anywhere possible.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.910
That way we could keep our
targeted goals in place.

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:02.510
Early on, we partnered with
Google's professional services

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:04.190
organization.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:09.662
They helped us move along that
journey to migrate to GCP,

00:06:09.662 --> 00:06:11.370
We do this in a couple
of different ways.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.370
So what we did in
the beginning is

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.640
we set up a bunch of
in-depth workshops with our

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.250
on premise support teams.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.890
That gave us the ability
to identify how Colgate

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:24.110
operates on a day to day.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:28.130
From there, Google was able to
produce a technical gold design

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:32.240
document, and that became our
true roadmap or our blueprint

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:33.770
going forward on
how we're actually

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:36.530
going to migrate to GCP.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:38.840
Now within that TDD,
it was broken up

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:41.720
into sections of the project,
and that-- those sections were

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:42.960
called work streams.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.710
We're going to go
over them in a second.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.460
So the work streams were
broken up into four key areas.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.720
Building the foundation was
a key part of our project.

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.410
It allowed us to build
all the technical building

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:02.180
blocks that were needed
before we migrated to GCP.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:05.090
Second, we migrated
a pilot workload.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.180
This gave our teams
the early access

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:13.440
to understand how
a migration works.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.610
And then in parallel, we also
implemented a data center

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:20.180
discovery tool that analyzed
all of our connections

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:22.910
within our data center so we
could understand what servers

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:25.590
are communicating to others.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:29.630
And then our final work stream
was our full Windows workload

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:30.543
migration.

00:07:30.543 --> 00:07:32.210
So for this presentation,
we're actually

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:35.810
going to focus on the first
and third work streams.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:42.120
So let's dig a little deeper
into those foundational

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:45.430
elements I spoke about earlier.

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:48.810
So the foundation really
consisted of three parts--

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:52.390
cloud governance, understanding
what governance policies

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:55.770
we needed to continue to do
in GCP while also building

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:59.550
new policies that were
more cloud focused.

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:00.550
Building a landing zone.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:02.520
So this is the technical
building blocks

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.770
I spoke about earlier
and all the pieces that

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:09.153
were a part of that in
order to migrate to GCP.

00:08:09.153 --> 00:08:11.070
And then we needed to
bring in some new tools,

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.570
and these tools were going to
help us go along that journey.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:18.870
So in the area of
cloud governance,

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:21.840
a couple of questions
were asked early on

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.250
such as which servers
could be migrated

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.200
and how are we going
to manage them.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.930
How are we going to secure
them, and how are we

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:34.150
going to control cost?

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.840
So in the area of
cloud readiness,

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:41.713
we had to come up with
a way to identify how--

00:08:41.713 --> 00:08:43.380
we had to come up
with a way to identify

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:44.940
that our servers were ready.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.040
So it's a couple of examples--

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.880
understanding how the license
structure of an application

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.260
could translate from on
premise to cloud, understanding

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:58.830
if the application
support from the vendor

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.620
carried over to the
same level of support

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.380
that we had on premise.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:08.610
That was a key prior
migrating to GCP.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:12.630
Also understanding the
amount of communication

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:16.170
that servers were having to
each other on premise also

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:20.420
was important to understand
if they were cloud ready.

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:23.910
In the area of infrastructure
management, what we had to do

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.920
there is understand
that we were going

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:30.180
to continue to use some of
our on premise applications

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:33.150
in the areas of patch
management and load

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.760
balancing and monitoring.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.770
And we identified
this because we

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:40.230
wanted to ensure that
we didn't have a reduced

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.720
functionality of what
we currently have,

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.220
but also we wanted
to also continue

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:47.820
to leverage our on premise--
our company's team's

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:51.030
expertise and the tools
that they already know.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.640
Now on the flip side
to that, we are also

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.930
continuing to investigate more
cloud-focused solutions to help

00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:59.490
us along our long-term journey.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:03.170
Platform security.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:06.300
So Colgate is G Suite company.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.720
So being able to integrate
with our current G Suite

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:12.860
implementation, we
created custom groups

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:15.470
that made access to
our Google Platform

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:19.040
for our support teams
quick and efficient.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:21.530
But then for our users,
we were able to use

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:24.980
the power of IAM or identity
and access management

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:27.830
to give our users the ability
to be agile in the cloud

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:30.560
but also control some
more sensitive areas

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:33.440
that we needed
more control over.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.040
And then finally
cost management.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.860
We had to come up
with a way that once

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:41.150
we migrated to GCP, how were
we going to manage costs?

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:45.380
So we did that in a
couple of different ways.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.440
By exporting our billing
information from GCP

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:51.470
to BigQuery, we were able to
leverage tools such as Data

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:53.870
Studio to build
custom dashboards

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:58.970
to give us the needed visibility
into our cost for our projects.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:04.310
And then an area of control
we used billing alerts

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:08.360
within the GCP console
to notify us that when

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:11.230
projects reach a certain
overage in terms of their cost

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:12.057
structure.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:18.690
So want to go a
little bit deeper

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.180
into the network
architecture and what we had

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:26.280
to do to be ready for Cloud.

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:28.845
We knew early on that we
had to keep connectivity

00:11:28.845 --> 00:11:32.040
back to on prem with
our cloud environment,

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:33.480
that hybrid connectivity.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:34.980
So-- and we also
wanted to make sure

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.470
that we set up our
isolated networks in a way

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:40.120
that we were as
secure as possible.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.810
So originally we set up-- we
did this through multiple VPCs

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:46.810
for both our nonprod and
production environments.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.400
We also configured VPNs for that
hybrid connectivity to multiple

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:51.630
regions in the cloud .

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:56.040
That way we could leverage
that global GCP network.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:57.420
And then in a high--

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:00.720
in our higher traffic areas,
we implemented interconnects

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:05.260
to ensure that that
bandwidth is increased.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:10.540
Now for our GCP communication,
we implemented shared VPCs

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:14.080
to enable our projects
within the same environment

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.610
to communicate to each other.

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:20.110
But in doing so we also
implemented micro segmentation.

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:23.080
That way servers could
communicate to other servers

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:25.060
only when we allow
it even if it's

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:27.190
sitting within the same subnet.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:29.680
That gave us the highest level
of security moving forward.

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:36.040
So let's talk about
some of the tools

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:38.950
that we're using to help us
with our migration journey.

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:45.280
So ATAVision is an
agentless discovery tool

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:48.380
that we currently have
deployed in our data center.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:53.260
It is helping us map our entire
server footprint of our data

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:57.320
center, and it's doing that
through a couple of key

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.500
is providing us a
couple of key things

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:03.220
in terms of technical
details so as I said,

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:05.320
server to server communication.

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:08.620
But how often is that
server communicating

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:10.180
to that other server.

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:12.970
That could help us identify
some of our top talkers

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:15.190
within our data center.

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:16.930
And as their eyes
are communicating

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:19.950
to each other, what kind of
protocols are they using--

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:20.920
TCP, UDP?

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:24.150
Is it inbound outbound traffic?

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:25.660
What ports are they using?

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:28.420
So implementing something
like micro segmentation,

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:30.130
you need to have
all that information

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:31.255
prior migrating to GCP.

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:37.180
At the end of it, having
that data center footprint

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:40.210
allowed us to create something
called migration move groups.

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:42.250
And what that basically
means is servers

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:46.550
that should move together
get moved together.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:49.330
So for Cloud security
and compliance,

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:52.600
we are leveraging Security
Command Center along

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:55.150
with Forseti Security.

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:57.010
And what that's basically
allowing us to do

00:13:57.010 --> 00:14:00.160
is put in all the
necessary rules in place

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:02.170
to make our GCP
environment more secure.

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:07.360
Once those rules
are in place, we're

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:12.430
not only able to sniff out
vulnerabilities of areas in GCP

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:15.220
but prevent them from
happening in the first place.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:17.980
Other areas such as
organizational policies,

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.650
that allows us the
highest level of

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:23.620
controls to be put in place
that are then hierarchied down

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:25.660
to all of our service projects.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:28.660
So I want to talk a little
bit about server migration.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:32.810
The tool that we are currently
using is called Velostrata.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:36.160
So once your hybrid
connectivity is in place

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:39.652
from your on prem environment
to your data center,

00:14:39.652 --> 00:14:41.110
you basically have
four major steps

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:43.330
that you need to do when
using the Velastrata tool.

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:48.070
So step 1 would be to
implement your management

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:49.190
pieces of Velastrata.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:52.610
So what that is, is an
appliance in your data center,

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:56.890
And That is the rightly working
with your VMware environment.

00:14:56.890 --> 00:14:59.110
And then you have
your compute instances

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:00.730
running in the GCP side.

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:04.240
Now these two
pieces of Velastrata

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:07.490
are in constant sync
with each other.

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:10.420
They allow basically the
entire migration process

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:14.890
that we're going to talk
about on this slide to occur.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:17.270
So once your management
pieces are in place,

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:20.050
we can begin the migration.

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:22.240
So on prem within
your V center--

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:26.530
your VMware environment, you're
able to execute a migration.

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:29.950
And what that's doing is
it's going to take the server

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:32.590
configuration that
you have on prem

00:15:32.590 --> 00:15:35.868
and replicate it in
the GCP environment.

00:15:35.868 --> 00:15:38.410
But you do have the option to
change that configuration based

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:40.480
on different needs.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:45.070
Maybe that server on prem was
sized at a higher workload

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:47.830
than it needed to be.

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:50.770
Within a short time,
30 minutes or so,

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:53.480
the GCP instances
are accessible.

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.840
So that's a key point
here because you

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:59.080
don't have to have an
excessive amount of downtime.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:02.173
You could turn over these
instances to your support teams

00:16:02.173 --> 00:16:03.590
or your application
teams to begin

00:16:03.590 --> 00:16:06.610
an additional configuration
or to just begin testing

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:09.320
of that application in GCP.

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:13.600
In the meantime, storage
migration begins.

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:17.470
So the on premise
disks will then

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:19.880
start to begin
streaming-- or the move--

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:24.923
the migrating of storage to
new disks within the GCP side.

00:16:24.923 --> 00:16:26.590
Now depending on the
size of those disks

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:31.330
will correlate to the length
of that disk migration.

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:33.250
Once a storage
migration is finished,

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:35.450
you're basically ready
to do your last step.

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:36.880
And that step is
called a detach.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:40.690
And a detach is basically
disconnecting your

00:16:40.690 --> 00:16:43.300
on premise environment
from your GCP environment

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:46.030
in terms of Velastrata,
and those disks

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:48.010
that are now fully
migrated are now

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:50.470
attached to those instances.

00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:51.330
And you're done.

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:53.650
That in a nutshell
is your four steps

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:57.610
on how to migrate your on
premise servers to GCP.

00:16:57.610 --> 00:16:59.500
I have a couple slides
here just to wrap up

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:01.560
my part of the presentation.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:03.970
I just want to talk about
some integration points

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:07.210
to consider when
possibly migrating

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:09.359
your environment to GCP.

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:12.220
So in terms of
firewall management

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:15.230
going to a GCP environment or
a public cloud environment,

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:19.190
you may want to consider a more
granular firewall management

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:21.530
such as micro segmentation.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:23.619
But when doing so, it is
critical to understand

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:26.319
how your on premise
environment is communicating

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:29.680
like I mentioned
from our conversation

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:32.110
before about ATAVision.

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:34.260
In terms of patch
management, we might

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.420
want to consider a new
patch management strategy

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:38.140
when going to the cloud.

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:41.040
This is a great time
to think about that.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:46.740
Having a pre-migration-- a
detailed pre-migration plan

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:51.420
where you could identify
some of the things that

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.510
might get missed during
a migration is key.

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:56.940
So, yes, we have
our ATAVision data.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:58.860
That's our technical
data, but being

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:01.170
able to supplement it
with something that we

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:03.240
do, such as application
interviews where

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:06.030
we speak to the application
owners and get some insights,

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:09.520
that maybe a tool can't give us.

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:11.730
So being able to take
that technical detail

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:14.970
and that app owner
detail, put it together,

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:17.190
gives us a higher chance
or a high confidence

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:18.667
when migrating to GCP.

00:18:18.667 --> 00:18:20.250
So we don't miss
something, let's say,

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:23.280
an application I might be
leveraging a load balancer.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:24.240
IP address mapping.

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:28.440
So when we are implementing
hybrid environment where you're

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:33.053
connecting your GCP environment
to your on premise environment,

00:18:33.053 --> 00:18:35.220
you want to make sure that
those subnets that you're

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:39.675
provisioning in GCP don't
conflict with your subnets

00:18:39.675 --> 00:18:41.550
that you already have
within your data center

00:18:41.550 --> 00:18:44.700
or globally for your company.

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:46.830
On the application
side, you want

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:50.220
to make sure that
you understand how

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:53.790
your applications are currently
interfacing on premise.

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:56.190
That's going to be key
to understand how you're

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.560
going to move not
just one application

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:01.200
but many applications at
a time to keep those move

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:03.310
groups together.

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:06.060
You also want to make sure
that your external storage

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:11.120
that your applications may use
stays intact post migration.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:15.240
And then having a very good
detailed cloud compatibility

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:15.990
check--

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:19.080
that's that cloud readiness--

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:21.640
could help you avoid some
potential issues in the future.

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:24.650
Let's talk about some
post-migration benefits.

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:27.180
So a lot of benefits
that you hear usually

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:28.885
come around a net
new environment.

00:19:28.885 --> 00:19:30.510
Someone is building
that new, and these

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:33.700
are all the benefits that you
get from running in the cloud.

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:37.410
But a lot of those benefits
also correlate over

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:40.260
to someone that's migrating
their servers to the cloud.

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:44.970
So live migration, being able
to reduce your-- or potentially

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:48.660
reduce your maintenance
windows by not having

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:52.440
to patch the physical hardware
because the live migration

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:55.180
is seamless to the customer.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:59.010
Right sizing, leveraging
Google's recommendation engine

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:01.860
and artificial
intelligence to understand

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:06.550
when servers are
underutilized or overutilized

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:10.320
and then provision the correct
sizing for those servers.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:13.250
Something even as simple
as hardware leases.

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:15.000
When migrating to the
cloud, you no longer

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:17.460
have to worry about
your hardware leases

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:19.820
and managing those schedules.

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:23.130
Faster provisioning,
not having to worry

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:26.490
about capacity planning,
which every data center has

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:30.510
to worry about, and using the
agility and the scalability

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:33.270
of the Cloud
Platform to provision

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:36.720
faster and basically on demand.

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:39.160
And then finally reduce
license management.

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:43.990
So leveraging Google's images
such as Windows and SQL server,

00:20:43.990 --> 00:20:46.110
you no longer have to
worry about those licenses

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:50.310
and managing those licenses cost
because they are built into the

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:51.760
build itself.

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:55.560
So in a nutshell, GCP is
helping us as a company

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:57.690
to become more agile.

00:20:57.690 --> 00:21:01.110
Google's professional
services are continuing

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:04.440
to help us grow in that sector.

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:06.090
And then the tools
that we're using

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:10.160
are helping us to get
to our destination.

